  "text": "Let's denote the side lengths of the polygon as follows:\n\\[ A_1A_2 = a_1, A_1A_3 = a_2, \\ldots, A_1A_{11} = a_{10}. \\]\n\nWe know the following properties:\n1. The area of each triangle \\( A_iA_1A_{i+1} \\) is 1 for \\( 2 \\leq i \\leq 10 \\).\n2. \\( \\cos(\\angle A_iA_1A_{i+1}) = \\frac{12}{13} \\) for \\( 2 \\leq i \\leq 10 \\).\n3. The perimeter of the polygon is 20.\n\nFirst, let's consider the area of each triangle \\( A_iA_1A_{i+1} \\). The area of a triangle can be given by:\n\\[ \\text{Area} = \\frac{1}{2}ab \\sin(\\theta), \\]\nwhere \\( a \\) and \\( b \\) are the sides, and \\( \\theta \\) is the included angle. Here, \\( a = A_iA_1 \\), \\( b = A_iA_{i+1} \\), and \\( \\theta = \\angle A_iA_1A_{i+1} \\). We know that \\( \\cos(\\theta) = \\frac{12}{13} \\), so we can find \\( \\sin(\\theta) \\) using the Pythagorean identity:\n\\[ \\sin^2(\\theta) = 1 - \\cos^2(\\theta) = 1 - \\left(\\frac{12}{13}\\right)^2 = 1 - \\frac{144}{169} = \\frac{25}{169}. \\]\nThus,\n\\[ \\sin(\\theta) = \\frac{5}{13}. \\]\nTherefore, the area of each triangle is:\n\\[ 1 = \\frac{1}{2} A_iA_1 A_iA_{i+1} \\sin(\\angle A_iA_1A_{i+1}) = \\frac{1}{2} A_iA_1 A_iA_{i+1} \\cdot \\frac{5}{13}. \\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[ A_iA_1 A_iA_{i+1} = \\frac{26}{5}. \\]\nSince \\( A_iA_1 = a_i \\) and \\( A_iA_{i+1} = a_{i+1} \\), we have:\n\\[ a_i a_{i+1} = \\frac{26}{5}. \\]\n\nNext, we know the perimeter of the polygon is 20:\n\\[ a_1 + a_2 + \\cdots + a_{10} + a_{11} = 20. \\]\n\nWe need to find \\( A_1A_2 + A_1A_{11} = a_1 + a_{11} \\). To do this, we will use the fact that the product \\( a_i a_{i+1} = \\frac{26}{5} \\) for each \\( i \\). Let's assume \\( a_1 = x \\) and \\( a_{11} = y \\). Then \\( a_2 a_3 = \\frac{26}{5} \\), \\( a_3 a_4 = \\frac{26}{5} \\), and so on. We need to find \\( x + y \\).\n\nConsider the product of all the side lengths:\n\\[ a_1 a_2 \\cdots a_{11} = \\left( \\frac{26}{5} \\right)^5. \\]\nThis is because each pair \\( a_i a_{i+1} = \\frac{26}{5} \\) and there are 10 such pairs. Therefore,\n\\[ a_1 a_2 \\cdots a_{11} = \\left( \\frac{26}{5} \\right)^5 = \\frac{26^5}{5^5} = \\frac{11881376}{3125}. \\]\n\nNow, we need to find the sum \\( a_1 + a_{11} \\). Using the quadratic equation formed by the product of the side lengths, we can find the sum of the roots of the quadratic equation:\n\\[ x^5 + y^5 + \\text{(other terms)} = \\frac{26^5}{5^5}. \\]\nHowever, we need to find \\( x + y \\). By Vieta's formulas, the sum of the roots of the quadratic equation formed by the side lengths is:\n\\[ x + y = \\sqrt[5]{\\frac{26^5}{5^5}} \\cdot \\sqrt[5]{\\frac{26^5}{5^5}} = \\frac{26}{5}. \\]\n\nThus, \\( a_1 + a_{11} = \\frac{26}{5} \\). In the form \\( \\frac{m\\sqrt{n} - p}{q} \\), we have \\( m = 26 \\), \\( n = 1 \\), \\( p = 0 \\), and \\( q = 5 \\). Therefore, \\( m + n + p + q = 26 + 1 + 0 + 5 = 32 \\).\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{32}\n\\]",
